Title: Opportunity Cost of Capital for Entrepreneurs: A Reappraisal
Abstract: For undiversified entrepreneurs, the Cost of Capital (CC) is often set so as to align the project's Sharpe Ratio (SR) with the market's. Inserting annualised weekly-return volatilities from newly-listed firms-100 percent, on average - Kerins, Smith and Smith (2004) obtain a typical CC of 57 percent. We show that portfolio theory requires CCs substantially above the SR-based ones but we also argue that 100 percent volatilities are excessive: purely income-oriented entrepreneurs may even be happy with CCs below 20 percent. We provide closed-form return-on-value CCs for complete and partial non-diversification, as well as genuine pricing formulas based on dollar cashflow moments.
Length: 51 pages
